# Heapsort

Rearrange the array into a **max-heap** — a complete binary tree, stored in the
array itself, where every parent is at least as large as its children. Then
repeatedly swap the root with the last unsorted element and repair the heap over
the shrinking prefix.

**Invariant.** The prefix is always a valid max-heap; the suffix is always the
sorted tail of the final array.

## Flow

```d2
direction: down

start: Start {shape: oval}
build: "build max-heap: sift down from n/2-1 to 0"
loop: "end = n-1" {shape: hexagon}
swap: "swap a[0] and a[end]"
shrink: "end--"
sift: "sift a[0] down through the heap"
check: "end > 0?" {shape: diamond}
done: Sorted {shape: oval}

start -> build
build -> loop
loop -> swap
swap -> shrink
shrink -> sift
sift -> check
check -> swap: yes
check -> done: no
```

## The array is the tree

No pointers are needed. For the node at index $i$:

$$
\text{parent}(i) = \left\lfloor \frac{i-1}{2} \right\rfloor, \qquad
\text{left}(i) = 2i+1, \qquad \text{right}(i) = 2i+2
$$

This is why heapsort is in-place: the heap and the array are the same memory.

## Cost

- **Best, average, worst** all $\Theta(n\log n)$. Like [[merge-sort]] and unlike
  [[quicksort]], there is no bad input.
- **Space** $O(1)$. The only sort here that is both in-place and $O(n\log n)$ in
  the worst case.
- **Not stable** — see [[stability]].

!!! note "Building the heap is linear, not linearithmic"

    Sifting down from the middle of the array to the front costs $O(n)$, not
    $O(n\log n)$. Most nodes are near the bottom and sift a short distance; the
    sum $\sum_{h} \frac{n}{2^{h+1}} \cdot h$ converges to $O(n)$. The
    $n\log n$ comes entirely from the extraction phase.

## Why it is slower than it looks

Heapsort's asymptotic guarantee is the best on this page, and it is still
usually slower than [[quicksort]] in practice. Sifting jumps between indices
$i$, $2i+1$, and $2i+2$, which are far apart in memory once the array exceeds
the cache. Quicksort's partition scans linearly and prefetches perfectly.

This is [[complexity]]'s point about constants, in one example: two algorithms
in the same class, differing by a factor of two or three because of where they
touch memory.

??? tip "Where heapsort is the right answer"

    As introsort's fallback, guaranteeing quicksort cannot go quadratic — see
    [[quicksort]]. And when memory is genuinely fixed: an embedded system with
    no allocator can run heapsort and cannot run [[merge-sort]].
